<Base title="About"><header class="page-head"><p class="eyebrow">Yes, the weird versions are staying</p><h1>About the project</h1><p class="lead">They Forgot the Alamo is my unfinished, low-poly browser game about a man who wakes up convinced he's Davy Crockett. This is where I'm keeping track of how it's taking shape.</p></header><section class="section"><h2>How it's being made</h2><p>This is my project: I set the direction, decide what I want, test builds, react to what I see, and ask for changes. AI coding tools do much of the coding and technical implementation. The shotgun has received an unreasonable number of notes about how Davy should hold it.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>Where things stand</h2><p>The playable build is one room. You can move around, aim, fire, reload, break parts of the room, and swap into a raccoon's point of view. It is still a prototype; the premise is well ahead of any campaign.</p><p>The notes are based on commits, screenshots, model tests, and other project records. When an old version is rebuilt to get a screenshot, the archive says so. The oldest saved game version I can verify is from September 29, 2026. That's the oldest receipt I have, not necessarily the day I first started thinking about the game.</p></section></Base>
